/code-review high 2026-10-08-release-tasks-bump

1. .config/mise/tasks/p/plugins/release:174 — A release target can fall below the manifest, and no task refuses it. This is the plan's own open gap G1; p:i:release and p:site:release share it. Scenario: level_implied reports only the highest differing component, and release_target then bumps from the last tag. With tag 21.0.0, manifest 21.2.0 and a recorded PATCH, the level is MINOR and the target is 21.1.0. Manifest 21.1.5 against tag 21.0.0 has the same problem. The node block then rewrites plugin.json from 21.2.0 down to 21.1.0, so the task silently lowers a version a human bumped. The test file asserts the happy paths only, and no case covers target < manifest.
2. .config/mise/tasks/p/release:103 — p:release never checks that gh is installed and authenticated before it merges and pushes. The tag-only steps that follow fail late and leave a local tag that blocks the resume. Scenario: p:release runs each task with --no-commit, which skips require_gh in p:site:release, and p:i:release only checks gh after tag_step. With gh missing or logged out, develop is bumped, committed, pushed and merged into main, and the plugin tags are pushed. mise run p:i:release --tag-only then creates installer-vX locally and dies at the gh check. The printed resume command, mise run p:i:release --tag-only, now fails with 'installer-vX already exists. Record a level...'. That remedy is wrong, because the right fix is to push the existing tag.
3. .config/mise/tasks/p/release:104 — Installer and site --tag-only block on gh run watch in the middle of p:release's step list. A pipeline failure stops the run with later tags uncreated, and the resume steps it prints cannot be re-run. Scenario: the order is plugins, then installer, then site. If the publish run of release.yml fails, p:i:release --tag-only exits non-zero. The tag is already created and pushed, and p:site:release --tag-only never ran. The Resume list starts with mise run p:i:release --tag-only, which now errors 'already exists' at the tag check, and the following site step is never reachable. Likewise, p:site:check runs after plugin and installer tags are public, so a failing site gate leaves a half-released product with develop already merged to main.
4. .config/mise/tasks/p/plugins/release:250 — The full flow calls code:merge:main and assumes it merged. Under MERGE_MODEL=pr the merge only opens a pull request and returns 0, so the task carries on tagging a stale main. Scenario: CLAUDE.md supports both landing models, and merge_to_destination_branch returns 0 after open_pull_request without merging. p:plugins:release then switches to the stale local main. tag_step finds every marketplace ref already tagged and prints 'nothing to tag', so it exits 0 and reports success although nothing shipped. p:i:release and p:site:release fail with 'already exists'. p:release prints 'Released: ...' while main never received the bump. No task refuses MERGE_MODEL=pr up front.
5. .config/mise/tasks/p/i/release:77 — p:i:release picks the last tag with git tag -l 'installer-v*' | head -n 1, with no plain X.Y.Z filter. The plugins and site tasks do filter. Scenario: a pre-release or other non-plain tag such as installer-v1.2.0-rc1 sorts ahead of the real latest tag. LAST then comes out as 1.2.0-rc1, which version_bump accepts after stripping the suffix, and the computed target and the 'already exists' check come out wrong. The two sibling tasks pin this with grep -E '^name-v[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$'.
6. .config/mise/tasks/p/site/release:186 — level_implied is called inside a nested command substitution outside the if ! guard, so with a manifest below the last tag even --dry-run exits non-zero instead of printing a 'refused' line. The same pattern is in p:plugins:release and p:i:release. Scenario: a manifest below the last tag makes level_implied return 1. level_max then gets an empty second argument and also fails, and set -e kills the task before the dry-run printf. The dry-run contract (E6) says a refusal prints '-> refused' and exits 0. Because mise run <task> --dry-run aborts, p:release --dry-run and its planning pass stop at the first affected project.
7. .github/workflows/deps-update.yml:71 — The monthly job replaces its hand-rolled merge with code:merge:main, which has preconditions the job never had to meet. Nothing in CI exercises them. Scenario: merge_to_destination_branch refuses on any untracked file, uncommitted change or unpushed-commit state. It also runs git pull origin main --tags and a pre-commit whole-tree pass if pre-commit is present. A stray untracked file from mise-action or pnpm in the runner would abort the job after the refresh commit is already pushed to develop, leaving develop carrying an unreleased installer PATCH record and no tag. Exercised only monthly.
8. .config/mise/tasks/p/i/release:81 — The treatment of an untagged project is inconsistent across the three tasks. p:i:release hard-fails with no installer tag, while p:plugins:release and p:site:release silently release the manifest as is, ignoring any recorded level. Scenario: for an untagged plugin or site, the task sets target = manifest and level = recorded, never calls release_target, and clears the key. A recorded MAJOR is therefore swallowed with no bump.
9. .config/mise/tasks/p/release:54 — p:release runs the three tasks' dry-runs once to plan and then runs each task again; the second pass can disagree with the first if a tag arrives on the remote in between, so the SUMMARY used as the commit message can differ from what the tasks bump.
10. CLAUDE.md:1 — The branch changes release-task behavior but leaves the repo docs stale (CLAUDE.md "The guard is four functions", the release skill, ci-and-releases.md). Until the plan's docs unit lands, the commits on this branch leave documented behavior false.
